      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The number of cans of a given size must increase linearly, and in this number $a-g+1$ they are numbered. \\[ \\begin{gathered}\n  a - 1 + 1 > 75 \\Rightarrow \\left( {a - 1} \\right) + 1 > 75 \\Rightarrow \\left( {a - 1 + 1} \\right) > 75 \\Rightarrow a \\, \\, \\left( {a + 1} \\right) > 100 \\, \\, \\left( {a + 10} \\right) > 100 \\hfill \\\\\n \\Rightarrow \\, \\, a + 10 > 100 \\Rightarrow a < 90 \\hfill \\\\\n \\Rightarrow  \\hfill \\left. \\begin{array}{l}\n {\\text{For}} \\left( {4 + \\frac{10}{{11}}} \\right)\\text{,} \\left( {9,10,11,12} \\right) \\hfill \\\\\n {\\sum\\limits\\limits_{m = 10}^{1000} {\\frac{{1000 - m}}{{11}}} \\leqslant 1000 - 10 = 990} \\hfill \\\\\n \\end{array} \\right\\}. \\hfill \\\\\n \\end{gathered} \\]\nThe display has 9 rows.\n\nTo solve the problem of determining how many rows the display of cans has, we need to analyze the pattern and use it to set up an inequality.\n\n1. **Identify the Pattern:**\n   - The top row has 1 can.\n   - Each lower row has 2 more cans than the row above it.\n\n2. **Define Variables:**\n   - Let \\( a \\) be the number of rows.\n   - The number of cans in the \\( n \\)-th row can be expressed as \\( 1 + 2(n-1) \\).\n\n3. **Set Up the Sum:**\n   - The total number of cans is the sum of the cans in each row.\n   - The sum of the first \\( a \\) terms of the arithmetic sequence starting at 1 and having a common difference of 2 is given by:\n     \\[\n     S = \\sum_{n=1}^{a} (1 + 2(n-1))\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the sum:\n     \\[\n     S = \\sum_{n=1}^{a} (1 + 2n - 2) = \\sum_{n=1}^{a} (2n - 1)\n     \\]\n\n4. **Factor Out the 2:**\n   - Notice that \\( 2n - 1 \\) can be rewritten as \\( 2(n-1) + 1 \\):\n     \\[\n     S = \\sum_{n=1}^{a} (2(n-1) + 1)\n     \\]\n   - Distribute the sum:\n     \\[\n     S = 2 \\sum_{n=1}^{a} (n-1) + \\sum_{n=1}^{a} 1\n     \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the Sums:**\n   - The sum of \\( n-1 \\) from \\( 1 \\) to \\( a \\) is a triangular number:\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{n=1}^{a} (n-1) = \\sum_{k=0}^{a-1} k = \\frac{(a-1)a}{2}\n     \\]\n   - The sum of 1 from \\( 1 \\) to \\( a \\) is simply \\( a \\):\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{n=1}^{a} 1 = a\n     \\]\n   - Combine the results:\n     \\[\n     S = 2 \\left( \\frac{(a-1)a}{2} \\right) + a = a(a-1) + a = a^2 - a + a = a^2\n     \\]\n\n6. **Set Up the Inequality:**\n   - We know the display contains 100 cans:\n     \\[\n     a^2 \\leq 100\n     \\]\n\n7. **Solve the Inequality:**\n   - Take the square root of both sides:\n     \\[\n     a \\leq \\sqrt{100} = 10\n     \\]\n\n8. **Determine the Integer Solution:**\n   - Since \\( a \\) must be an integer, the largest possible value for \\( a \\) is 9.\n\nThus, the display contains 9 rows. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
